#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079296} :activity/id #uuid "b20c9f5a-b450-5954-8b2f-bbe839892fde" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048563 :topic/id #uuid "aa3e68fe-4581-52ac-895f-4225e07db905" :topic/math-academy-id 632} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079704 :tutorial/content "In addition to the properties that we've already discussed, there are several more properties that we can use to calculate definite integrals.\n\nThe first property states that if the lower and upper limits of a definite integral are the same, then the integral evaluates to zero: \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\color{blue}a}^{\\color{blue}a} f(x) \\, \\textrm d x = 0.\n$$\n\nFor example, in the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\color{blue}3}^{\\color{blue}3} x \\, \\textrm d x ,\n$$\n\n the upper and lower limits of integration are the same. If we evaluate the integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{3}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere's an intuitive explanation for this result. The integral $\\displaystyle\\int_3^3 x \\, \\textrm d x$ can be thought of as the area under the graph of $y = x$ between $x=3$ and $x=3$, which is a rectangle of zero width. A rectangle of zero width has no area!" :tutorial/id #uuid "26d18176-2077-5153-8ab9-e60785e25e5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 779 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "86e79d43-4ad8-54e9-9f94-9f653c6a1746" :step/math-academy-id 1492 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078425 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078290 :question/id #uuid "c02d2000-3fef-59e4-9127-84500ef83eb8" :question/math-academy-id "e-418"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050057}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364323}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"} :step/id #uuid "141c8f26-4787-55fe-ac88-f25213bbe1e0" :step/math-academy-id 1494 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079298}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079705 :tutorial/content "The second property states that reversing the limits of integration changes the sign of the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\color{red}a}^{\\color{blue}b} f(x)\\, \\textrm d x = - \\int_{\\color{blue}b}^{\\color{red}a} f(x)\\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nSo, for example, if we know that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\color{red}1}^{\\color{blue}2} 2x\\, \\textrm d x =3,\n$$\n\n \n\nthen we can immediately conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\color{blue}2}^{\\color{red}1} 2x\\, \\textrm d x = -3.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e62a5fc4-17c9-5afb-bfea-b3d72535936e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 782 :tutorial/title "Reversing the Limits of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "76a21cf7-c7f3-58ba-b746-c866d1d0e6be" :step/math-academy-id 1497 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079299}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078426 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078291 :question/id #uuid "c7a90d29-e8b9-5a06-94f7-776eb254cc52" :question/math-academy-id "e-419"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385309}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "86f99e5a-7017-53e2-99de-68e1d35694ab" :step/math-academy-id 1498 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079300}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079706 :tutorial/content "The last property states that if we have the sum of two integrals with the same integrand, and where the lower bound of one integral matches the upper bound of the other integral, such as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^{\\color{blue}0} 4x^2\\, \\textrm d x + \\int_{\\color{blue}0}^{5} 4x^2\\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nthen we can combine the two integrals into a single integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-3}^{\\color{blue}0} 4x^2\\, \\textrm d x + \\int_{\\color{blue}0}^{5} 4x^2\\, \\textrm d x = \\int_{-3}^{5} 4x^2\\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\int_a^c f(x) \\, \\textrm d x + \\int_c^b f(x) \\, \\textrm d x = \\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo understand the reasoning behind this rule, remember that the integrals represent area. So, the rule states that the area bounded by a function on the interval $[a,b]$ is equal to the sum of areas on the adjacent intervals $[a,c]$ and $[c,b].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0dbaffac-ad36-5df8-898b-ef366cd640ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 784 :tutorial/title "The Integral of a Function Over Adjacent Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "e72a483a-204e-5b98-9edc-e094d1b7d15c" :step/math-academy-id 1500 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078427 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078295 :question/id #uuid "a85f51c5-601b-52ec-82dd-0b611842d3d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-420"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416140}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "aa3bf8cd-0e62-5b48-a131-0fc027187804" :step/math-academy-id 1501 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078428 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078304 :question/id #uuid "3d925b89-dc4d-59e0-bf00-3a3ca90b5169" :question/math-academy-id "e-421"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362853}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"} :step/id #uuid "05a7e09c-1b45-5d1a-adf8-02c9162f1406" :step/math-academy-id 1503}] :activity/title "Properties of Definite Integrals Involving the Limits of Integration" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078656}]}
